1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,253
Why does ice float in water?

2
00:00:02,253 --> 00:00:04,289
Why does a cut apple turn brown?

3
00:00:04,289 --> 00:00:07,248
And where does salt go
when it vanishes in water?

4
00:00:07,649 --> 00:00:09,113
Three small puzzles.

5
00:00:09,113 --> 00:00:11,213
One science answers all three.

6
00:00:11,663 --> 00:00:13,640
Diya meets them in her kitchen.

7
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,889
Ice weighs less than the
same space filled with water.

8
00:00:17,239 --> 00:00:19,801
The apple reacts with oxygen in the air.

9
00:00:19,801 --> 00:00:22,991
And the salt breaks into
particles too tiny to see.

10
00:00:23,491 --> 00:00:25,099
That science is chemistry.

11
00:00:25,099 --> 00:00:25,999
Welcome!

12
00:00:25,737 --> 00:00:27,947
Let's build the ground floor together.

13
00:00:28,597 --> 00:00:30,003
So what is chemistry?

14
00:00:30,353 --> 00:00:32,649
Chemistry is the study of matter.

15
00:00:32,649 --> 00:00:36,154
Matter is anything that has
mass and takes up space.

16
00:00:36,154 --> 00:00:38,236
Your pen, water, even air.

17
00:00:38,586 --> 00:00:42,920
Chemistry asks what matter is made
of, how its parts are arranged, how

18
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:45,083
it behaves, and how it changes.

19
00:00:45,683 --> 00:00:47,480
Matter comes in three states.

20
00:00:47,830 --> 00:00:51,672
In a solid, like ice, particles
are packed in neat rows.

21
00:00:51,672 --> 00:00:53,441
They only shake in place.

22
00:00:53,841 --> 00:00:58,488
In a liquid, like water, particles
stay close but slide past each other.

23
00:00:58,488 --> 00:00:59,479
So it flows.

24
00:00:59,879 --> 00:01:05,175
In a gas, like steam, particles are far
apart and fly fast in every direction.

25
00:01:05,775 --> 00:01:08,183
Now let's zoom in, one step at a time.

26
00:01:08,533 --> 00:01:12,418
Matter of just one kind is a
pure substance, like pure water.

27
00:01:12,818 --> 00:01:16,116
An element has only one
kind of atom, like oxygen.

28
00:01:16,516 --> 00:01:21,235
A compound has two or more kinds of
atoms, joined in a fixed ratio, like

29
00:01:21,235 --> 00:01:22,135
water.

30
00:01:22,241 --> 00:01:27,015
A molecule is the smallest bit of a
substance that can exist on its own.

31
00:01:27,015 --> 00:01:29,387
And molecules are built from atoms.

32
00:01:29,787 --> 00:01:33,701
Inside each atom sit protons,
neutrons and electrons.

33
00:01:33,701 --> 00:01:36,254
We open the atom in chapter two.

34
00:01:36,854 --> 00:01:40,057
Let's build molecules
on our molecule bench.

35
00:01:40,057 --> 00:01:42,272
Two oxygen atoms come close.

36
00:01:42,622 --> 00:01:45,199
Each puts two electrons in the middle.

37
00:01:45,199 --> 00:01:47,674
Two shared pairs make a double bond.

38
00:01:47,674 --> 00:01:49,679
One oxygen molecule, O two.

39
00:01:50,079 --> 00:01:52,051
Two atoms, but one kind.

40
00:01:52,051 --> 00:01:53,917
So oxygen is an element.

41
00:01:54,317 --> 00:01:58,738
Now two hydrogen atoms each share
one electron with an oxygen.

42
00:01:58,738 --> 00:02:00,216
Water, H two O.

43
00:02:00,616 --> 00:02:04,252
And carbon shares two pairs
with each of two oxygens.

44
00:02:04,252 --> 00:02:06,175
Carbon dioxide, CO two.

45
00:02:06,175 --> 00:02:09,531
Different kinds of atoms,
so both are compounds.

46
00:02:10,181 --> 00:02:11,557
Now read a formula.

47
00:02:11,557 --> 00:02:14,347
Each capital letter
starts an element symbol.

48
00:02:14,347 --> 00:02:16,812
NA is sodium, from its Latin name.

49
00:02:17,562 --> 00:02:21,550
A small number at the lower
right counts the atom before it.

50
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:24,403
H two O has two hydrogens and one oxygen.

51
00:02:25,153 --> 00:02:27,061
No small number means one.

52
00:02:27,061 --> 00:02:29,950
NACL is one sodium and one chlorine.

53
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:33,883
A big number in front
counts whole molecules.

54
00:02:33,883 --> 00:02:36,531
Two H two O is two water molecules.

55
00:02:37,281 --> 00:02:41,614
A small number after a bracket
multiplies everything inside it.

56
00:02:41,614 --> 00:02:46,619
In calcium hydroxide, the two doubles
both the oxygen and the hydrogen.

57
00:02:47,369 --> 00:02:48,618
Side by side.

58
00:02:48,618 --> 00:02:50,955
O two has one kind of atom.

59
00:02:50,955 --> 00:02:52,932
H two O has two kinds.

60
00:02:53,332 --> 00:02:54,550
Pause and think.

61
00:02:54,550 --> 00:02:56,956
Is O two an element or a compound?

62
00:03:00,156 --> 00:03:01,112
An element!

63
00:03:01,112 --> 00:03:03,461
Two atoms, but both are oxygen.

64
00:03:03,461 --> 00:03:07,566
Only different kinds of atoms
joined together make a compound.

65
00:03:08,216 --> 00:03:10,449
Chemistry is also about change.

66
00:03:10,449 --> 00:03:12,033
Look at two changes.

67
00:03:12,383 --> 00:03:15,016
Ice melts, but it is still H two O.

68
00:03:15,016 --> 00:03:16,293
No new substance.

69
00:03:16,293 --> 00:03:17,619
A physical change.

70
00:03:18,019 --> 00:03:23,313
Iron in damp air joins with oxygen and
water to make rust, a new substance.

71
00:03:23,313 --> 00:03:24,722
A chemical change.

72
00:03:25,372 --> 00:03:26,411
Remember this.

73
00:03:26,411 --> 00:03:28,956
Everything around you is made of atoms.

74
00:03:28,956 --> 00:03:32,152
Chemistry is how they
join, and how they change.

75
00:03:32,852 --> 00:03:34,364
Here is our journey.

76
00:03:34,364 --> 00:03:36,383
Chapter one, basic concepts.

77
00:03:36,383 --> 00:03:38,616
Chapter two, structure of atom.

78
00:03:38,616 --> 00:03:41,001
Chapter three, the periodic table.

79
00:03:41,401 --> 00:03:43,405
Chemistry also has branches.

80
00:03:43,405 --> 00:03:47,476
Physical chemistry studies energy
and the speed of reactions.

81
00:03:47,476 --> 00:03:50,509
Organic chemistry, the
compounds of carbon.

82
00:03:50,909 --> 00:03:52,892
Inorganic, the other elements.

83
00:03:52,892 --> 00:03:55,870
Analytical, what is in
a sample and how much.

84
00:03:55,870 --> 00:03:58,925
And biochemistry, the
chemistry of living things.

85
00:03:59,575 --> 00:04:01,310
Now, one worked example.

86
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:06,814
How many atoms are in one molecule
of glucose, C six H twelve O six?

87
00:04:06,814 --> 00:04:10,328
Glucose is the sugar your
body uses for energy.

88
00:04:10,828 --> 00:04:11,728
Carbon.

89
00:04:11,645 --> 00:04:12,722
Six atoms.

90
00:04:13,472 --> 00:04:14,406
Hydrogen.

91
00:04:14,406 --> 00:04:15,659
Twelve atoms.

92
00:04:16,409 --> 00:04:17,332
Oxygen.

93
00:04:17,332 --> 00:04:18,548
Six atoms.

94
00:04:19,298 --> 00:04:22,450
Six plus twelve plus six
is twenty four atoms.

95
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,547
And two H two O?

96
00:04:24,547 --> 00:04:26,914
Three atoms each, so six in all.

97
00:04:27,564 --> 00:04:29,029
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:29,379 --> 00:04:32,586
Many students think two
H and H two are the same.

99
00:04:32,986 --> 00:04:34,168
They are not.

100
00:04:34,168 --> 00:04:36,926
Two H is two separate hydrogen atoms.

101
00:04:36,926 --> 00:04:40,582
H two is one molecule,
two atoms bonded together.

102
00:04:41,232 --> 00:04:42,844
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:42,844 --> 00:04:48,003
Counting atoms in a formula sits inside
almost every mole concept numerical.

104
00:04:48,603 --> 00:04:49,958
Exam level now.

105
00:04:49,958 --> 00:04:55,032
How many oxygen atoms are in two
formula units of aluminium sulphate?

106
00:04:55,032 --> 00:04:56,444
Pause and solve.

107
00:05:01,844 --> 00:05:03,317
The answer is D.

108
00:05:03,317 --> 00:05:04,424
Twenty four.

109
00:05:04,924 --> 00:05:06,966
SO four has four oxygens.

110
00:05:06,966 --> 00:05:11,198
The three after the bracket
makes twelve oxygens in one unit.

111
00:05:11,698 --> 00:05:13,580
The big two means two units.

112
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:15,650
Two times twelve is twenty four.

113
00:05:16,150 --> 00:05:17,676
B forgot the big two.

114
00:05:17,676 --> 00:05:20,968
A and C added four and three
instead of multiplying.

115
00:05:21,568 --> 00:05:23,027
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:23,027 --> 00:05:24,526
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:27,726 --> 00:05:30,725
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:05:31,125 --> 00:05:32,025
One.

119
00:05:31,638 --> 00:05:35,381
How do particles move in a
solid, a liquid and a gas?

120
00:05:36,181 --> 00:05:37,081
Two.

121
00:05:36,743 --> 00:05:40,653
Is carbon dioxide an element
or a compound, and why?

122
00:05:41,453 --> 00:05:42,353
Three.

123
00:05:42,045 --> 00:05:44,559
How many atoms are in three H two O?

124
00:05:45,459 --> 00:05:49,586
Next time: how do chemists turn
plain air into food for plants?

125
00:05:49,586 --> 00:05:52,755
We find out in importance
and scope of chemistry.
